[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (5) Subject to paragraphs (6) and (10), the fast track is the normal track for any claim—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (a)for which the small claims track is not the normal track; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (b)which—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (i)is a claim for monetary relief, the value of which is not more than £25,000; or

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (ii)is or includes a claim for non-monetary relief and—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (aa)if the claim includes a claim for monetary relief, the value of the claim for monetary relief is not more than £25,000;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (bb)the claim meets the criteria in paragraph (6)(a) and (b); and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (cc)the court is satisfied that it is in the interests of justice for it to be allocated to the fast track. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (6) The fast track is the normal track for the claims referred to in paragraph (5) only if the court considers that—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (a)the trial is likely to last for no longer than one day; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (b)oral expert evidence at trial is likely to be limited to—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (i)one expert per party in relation to any expert field; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (ii)expert evidence in two expert fields. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (7) Subject to paragraphs (8), (9) and (10), the intermediate track is the normal track where—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (a)the claim is suitable for neither the small claims track nor the fast track;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (b)the claim includes a claim for monetary relief, the value of which is not more than £100,000;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (c)the court considers that—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (i)if the case is managed proportionately, the trial will not last longer than three days;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (ii)oral expert evidence at trial is likely to be limited to two experts per party;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (iii)the claim may be justly and proportionately managed under the procedure set out in Section IV of Part 28; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (iv)there are no additional factors, which would make the claim inappropriate for the intermediate track; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (d)the claim is brought by one claimant against either one or two defendants, or is brought by two claimants against one defendant. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (8) Where the relief sought includes a claim for non-monetary relief, the claim shall not be allocated to the intermediate track unless the court also considers it to be in the interests of justice to do so. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (9) Subject to paragraph (10), the court may allocate a claim to the intermediate track where it considers it to be in the interests of justice to do so. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (10) A claim must be allocated to the multi-track where that claim is—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (a)a mesothelioma claim or asbestos lung disease claim;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (b)one which includes a claim for clinical negligence, unless—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (i)the claim is one which would normally be allocated to the intermediate track;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (ii)there has been an admission of liability in full, which means that the defendant accepts that the claimant has suffered loss, including the injury set out in the letter of claim under the Pre-Action Protocol for the Resolution of Clinical Disputes, caused by the defendant’s breach of duty of care; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (iii)the admission in paragraph (ii) is made in the defendant’s letter of response provided in accordance with the Pre-Action Protocol for the Resolution of Clinical Disputes,

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] provided that the defendant has not raised a defence to the claim under the Limitation Act 1980;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (c)a claim for damages in relation to harm, abuse or neglect of or by children or vulnerable adults;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (d)a claim is one the court could order to be tried by jury if satisfied that there is in issue a matter set out in section 66(3) of the County Courts Act 1984(3) or section 69(1) of the Senior Courts Act 1981(4);

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (e)a claim against the police which includes a claim for—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (i)an intentional or reckless tort; or

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (ii)relief or a remedy in relation to a breach of the Human Rights Act 1998(5); or

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (f)a claim against a public authority for trespass to the person, unless, having regard to the matters mentioned in rule 26.13(1), the court considers that it would not be in the interests of justice to do so. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (11) Paragraph (10)(e) does not apply to—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (a)a road accident claim arising from negligent police driving;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (b)an employer’s liability claim;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (c)any other claim for an accidental fall on police premises. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.9. —(1) The small claims track is the normal track for—] (12) The multi-track is the normal track for any claim for which the small claims track or the fast track or the intermediate track is not the normal track. ## Road traffic accident related personal injury claims

## 26.10. The circumstances referred to in rule 26.9(1)(a)(ii)(bb) are—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.10. The circumstances referred to in rule 26.9(1)(a)(ii)(bb) are—] (a)the accident occurred before 31st May 2021;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.10. The circumstances referred to in rule 26.9(1)(a)(ii)(bb) are—] (b)unless rule 26.11 applies, on the date that proceedings are started, the claimant is—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.10. The circumstances referred to in rule 26.9(1)(a)(ii)(bb) are—] (i)a child; or

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.10. The circumstances referred to in rule 26.9(1)(a)(ii)(bb) are—] (ii)a protected party;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.10. The circumstances referred to in rule 26.9(1)(a)(ii)(bb) are—] (c)when the accident occurred, the claimant was—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.10. The circumstances referred to in rule 26.9(1)(a)(ii)(bb) are—] (i)using a motor cycle;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.10. The circumstances referred to in rule 26.9(1)(a)(ii)(bb) are—] (ii)a pillion passenger on, or a passenger in a sidecar attached to, a motor cycle;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.10. The circumstances referred to in rule 26.9(1)(a)(ii)(bb) are—] (iii)using a wheelchair, a powered wheelchair or a mobility scooter;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.10. The circumstances referred to in rule 26.9(1)(a)(ii)(bb) are—] (iv)using a bicycle or other pedal cycle;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.10. The circumstances referred to in rule 26.9(1)(a)(ii)(bb) are—] (v)riding a horse; or

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.10. The circumstances referred to in rule 26.9(1)(a)(ii)(bb) are—] (vi)a pedestrian;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.10. The circumstances referred to in rule 26.9(1)(a)(ii)(bb) are—] (d)unless rule 26.11 applies, on the date that proceedings are started—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.10. The circumstances referred to in rule 26.9(1)(a)(ii)(bb) are—] (i)the claimant is an undischarged bankrupt; or

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.10. The circumstances referred to in rule 26.9(1)(a)(ii)(bb) are—] (ii)the claimant or defendant acts as a personal representative of a deceased person; or

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.10. The circumstances referred to in rule 26.9(1)(a)(ii)(bb) are—] (e)unless rule 26.11 applies, on the date of the accident, the defendant’s vehicle was registered outside the United Kingdom. ## Children and protected parties

## 26.11 .—(1) The fast track is the normal track where a claim—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.11 .—(1) The fast track is the normal track where a claim—] (a)is for personal injuries arising from a road traffic accident which occurs on or after 31st May 2021;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.11 .—(1) The fast track is the normal track where a claim—] (b)is made by—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.11 .—(1) The fast track is the normal track where a claim—] (i)a child or a protected party; or

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.11 .—(1) The fast track is the normal track where a claim—] (ii)a person who, on the date the claim was first presented via the Pre-Action Protocol for Low Value Personal Injury Claims in Road Traffic Accidents, was a child; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.11 .—(1) The fast track is the normal track where a claim—] (c)consists of, or includes, a claim for a whiplash injury. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.11 .—(1) The fast track is the normal track where a claim—] (2) Where this rule applies, the claim must not be allocated to the small claims track. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > 26.11 .—(1) The fast track is the normal track where a claim—] (3) ‘Whiplash injury’ has the meaning ascribed to it by paragraph 1.2(38) of the Pre-Action Protocol for Personal Injury Claims Below the Small Claims Limit in Road Traffic Accidents. ## Allocation – general

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Allocation – general] 26.12. —(1) In considering whether to allocate a claim to the normal track for that claim under rules 26.9, 26.10 or 26.11, the court shall have regard to the matters mentioned in rule 26.13(1). [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Allocation – general] (2) The court must not allocate a claim to the small claims track, if it includes a claim by a tenant of residential premises against his landlord for a remedy in respect of harassment or unlawful eviction. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Allocation – general] (3) In claims under the Renting Homes (Wales) Act 2016, the court must not allocate a claim to the small claims track if it includes a claim by a contract-holder of a dwelling against their landlord for a remedy in respect of harassment or unlawful eviction. ## Matters relevant to allocation to a track

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] 26.13 .—(1) When deciding the track for a claim, the matters to which the court shall have regard include—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (a)the financial value, if any, of the claim;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (b)the nature of the remedy sought;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (c)the likely complexity of the facts, law or evidence;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (d)the number of parties or likely parties;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (e)the value of any counterclaim or additional claim and the complexity of any matters relating to it;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (f)the amount of oral evidence which may be required;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (g)the importance of the claim to persons who are not parties to the proceedings;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (h)the views expressed by the parties; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (i)the circumstances of the parties. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (2) It is for the court to assess the financial value of a claim and in doing so it shall disregard—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (a)any amount not in dispute;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (b)any claim for interest;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (c)costs;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (d)any contributory negligence; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (e)where the claim is, or includes a claim for non-monetary relief, any amount prescribed by rule 45.45(1)(a)(ii) and rule 45.50(2)(b)(ii). [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (3) Where—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (a)two or more claimants have started a claim against the same defendant using the same claim form; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] (b)each claimant has a claim against the defendant separate from the other claimants,

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Matters relevant to allocation to a track] the court shall consider the claim of each claimant separately when it assesses financial value under paragraph (1). ## Assignment within the fast track and the intermediate track

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track and the intermediate track] 26.14. —(1) When a claim is allocated to the fast track or the intermediate track, the court must also assign the claim to a complexity band, unless it is one to which Section VIII of Part 45 applies. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track and the intermediate track] (2) In both Table 1 and Table 2, below, the complexity bands numbered 1 to 4 provide an ascending scale of allowable costs commensurate with the complexity of the claim. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track and the intermediate track] (3) The complexity band to which a claim is assigned shall determine the costs that are to be allowed under Table 12 or Table 14 in Practice Direction 45. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track and the intermediate track] (4) Subject to paragraph (5), the parties may agree the complexity band to which a claim is assigned. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track and the intermediate track] (5) The court may direct that a claim be assigned to a different complexity band than that agreed by the parties, but shall have regard to the factors set out in rule 26.13(1). [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track and the intermediate track] (6) A party must state on their directions questionnaire—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track and the intermediate track] (a)the agreed complexity band; or

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track and the intermediate track] (b)where the parties disagree, the complexity band considered appropriate by that party,

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track and the intermediate track] together with any relevant information in support. ## Assignment within the fast track

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track] 26.15. Unless the claim is one for noise induced hearing loss (in respect of which Sections I and IV of Part 28 and Section VIII of Part 45 make provision), the complexity band to which a claim will normally be assigned in the fast track is set out in Table 1. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track] Table 1

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track] (a) road traffic accident related, non-personal injury claims; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track] (a) road traffic accident related, personal injury claims which are or should have been started under the RTA Protocol; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track] (a) road traffic accident related, personal injury claims to which the RTA Protocol does not apply;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track] (a) employer’s liability disease claims (other than a claim for noise induced hearing loss);

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track] (b) defended debt claims

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track] (b) personal injury claims to which the Pre-action Protocol for Resolution of Package Travel Claims apply

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track] (b) employer’s liability (accident) and public liability personal injury claims;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track] (b) complex possession and housing disrepair claims;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track] (c) possession claims;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track] (c) property and building disputes;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track] (d) housing disrepair claims; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track] (d) professional negligence claims; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track] (e) other claims for a sum of money, whether the sum is specified or unspecified, except claims that fall under complexity band 1(b)

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the fast track] (e) any claim which would normally be allocated to the fast track, but is nonetheless complex

## Assignment within the intermediate track

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the intermediate track] 26.16. The complexity band to which a claim will normally be assigned in the intermediate track is set out in Table 2. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the intermediate track] Table 2

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the intermediate track] Any claim where—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the intermediate track] (a) only one issue is in dispute; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the intermediate track] Any more complex claim where more than one issue is in dispute, but which is unsuitable for assignment to complexity band 2, including noise induced hearing loss and other employer’s liability disease claims. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the intermediate track] Any claim which would normally be allocated to the intermediate track, but which is unsuitable for assignment to complexity bands 1 to 3, including any personal injury claim where there are serious issues of fact or law. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the intermediate track] (b) the trial is not expected to last longer than one day, including—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the intermediate track] (i) personal injury claims where liability or quantum is in dispute;

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Assignment within the intermediate track] (ii) road traffic accident related, non-personal injury claims; and

## Notice of allocation and assignment

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Notice of allocation and assignment] 26.17 . When it has allocated a claim to a track, the court shall serve notice of allocation and, where applicable, assignment on every party. ## Reallocation and reassignment

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Reallocation and reassignment] 26.18 .—(1) Subject to paragraphs (2) and (3), the court may on application or on its own initiative subsequently—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Reallocation and reassignment] (a)reallocate a claim to a different track; or

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Reallocation and reassignment] (b)reassign a claim to a different complexity band. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Reallocation and reassignment] (2) Where—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Reallocation and reassignment] (a)a claim is allocated to the intermediate track; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Reallocation and reassignment] (b)directions in respect of that claim have been given,

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Reallocation and reassignment] the court may only reallocate the claim where it decides that there are exceptional reasons to justify doing so. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Reallocation and reassignment] (3) The court may only reassign a claim to a different complexity band, where—

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Reallocation and reassignment] (a)there has been a change in circumstances since a direction was made assigning the claim to a particular complexity band; and

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Reallocation and reassignment] (b)the court decides the change in circumstances justifies reassignment. ## Trial with a jury

[PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Trial with a jury] 26.19 .—(1) An application for a claim, other than a claim for libel and slander, to be tried with a jury must be made within 28 days of service of the defence. [PART 26 – CASE MANAGEMENT – PRELIMINARY STAGE > Trial with a jury] (2) A claim for libel or slander must be tried by Judge alone, unless at the first case management conference a party applies for trial with a jury and the court makes an order to that effect. ## Footnotes

